                      tnx - thanks for the error report

  Syntax:     tnx=code,number,action,#num
              tnx=list
              tnx

  Response:   tnx/code,number,action,#num,internal,external


Settable parameters:
  code     Two character error code, e.g., 'sp', or 'list' to show the status
           of all "recognized" errors (see Comments about 'tnx=list').

  number   Error message number, e.g., '-999'.

  action   Display status: 'on', 'off', or 'add'. The default is 'off'. 'add'
           creates a default of 'off' for all errors with 'code,number' that
           have not been seen yet. See Comments about 'add'. In responses,
           only 'off' and 'on' can occur.

  #num     literal '#' followed by an integer associated with the error
           "string" to act on. A '#0' indicates a default for 'code,number'
           created by 'add'. Negative values match all instances of
           'code,number', including '#0'. Default is null (empty), which will
           select an only instance, '#1', of an error 'code,number', or if
           there is no '#1', but there is '#0',it will select '#0'. You can
           always specify a literal '#num' to remove any ambiguity.

Monitor-only parameters:
  internal The internal error message "string", in curly braces, '{}',
           associated with 'code,number' and '#num'. The string is null for
           '#0' or if the 'code,number' is NOT defined in 'fserr.ctl' or
           'sterr.ctl'. It can only be non-null if the string in 'external' is
           null. Any curly braces within 'string' are not escaped, but should
           be rare.

  external The external error message "string", in curly braces, '{}',
           associated with 'code,number' and '#num'. An external message is
           one not defined in 'fserr.ctl' or 'sterr.ctl'. The string is null
           for '#0' or if the 'code,number' is defined in 'fserr.ctl' or
           'sterr.ctl'. It can only be non-null if the string in 'internal' is
           null. Any curly braces within 'string' are not escaped, but should
           be rare.


Comments:
This command is intended to provide a way to turn off display of specific
error messages if the operator determines that nothing can be done to correct
the problem. For example, if one parameter of a module's set-up is being
reported incorrectly by the hardware, but has been checked and is known to be
correct, the operator can suppress that one error message rather than
unchecking the entire module. Even if the error message is suppressed for
display, it is still written to the log.

WARNING: This command must be used with great care. If misused, it may turn
off important error messages that the operator needs to know about.
Consequently, its use (except for just 'tnx' and 'tnx=list') is blocked if it
is executed from a procedure, schedule, or time-list. This command must be
entered interactively (in a pinch, the 'op_stream' command or the
'inject_snap' program can be used to workaround this restriction). The list of
suppressed error messages is cleared every time the FS is started. TURNING OFF
AN ERROR MESSAGE IS NOT THE SAME AS CORRECTING THE UNDERLYING PROBLEM, WHICH
IS THE PREFERRED METHOD OF DEALING WITH AN ISSUE.

If you use this command to suppress display of any error messages, it is
recommended that you place 'tnx' as the first command in the station
'sched_initi' procedure to record which messages are already being suppressed
at the start of each schedule.

The form 'tnx=list' will show the status of all "recognized" errors. The
recognized errors are all the encountered errors plus errors set with 'add'
regardless if they are currently being suppressed. The form 'tnx' shows only
suppressed messages.

Normally, an error must have occurred before its display status can be
manipulated with 'tnx'. However, for 'add' a "default" if of 'off' is created
for any instance of 'code,number' that have not already occurred. This provides
a way to turn off an error in advance if its being reported will cause a
problem for an unattended system (perhaps needlessly waking someone in the
middle-of-the-night). The "default" entry is shown as instance '#0' in 'tnx'
and 'tnx=list' output, when appropriate. The display status for new instances
can be manipulated by using 'on' and 'off' for '#0'.

If the 'action' parameter is 'on' display of the message is re-enabled.

Use of the "previous value" symbol "*" is not supported for any parameters of
"tnx".

The '#num' parameter is only needed if more than instance of an error with the
specified 'code,number' has occurred. If there is more than one instance (not
including '#0') and '#num' is not specified, all instances that have occurred
so far are displayed with their corresponding 'num' values along with a
suggestion to use the '#num' form. If '#num' is negative, it will match all
instances (including '#0' if it exists) of the 'code,number' that have
occurred so far. In this case only, the FS will not complain if the display
status of any of 'code,number' errors are already in the requested state.
